  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nA chaotic upper level flow regime is found over the northern \nPlains, with transient ridging building in up ahead of a closed \noff trough in the eastern Canadian Prairies. With high pressure \nsliding across the forecast area into South Dakota, light winds \nare anticipated to turn southerly through the afternoon and \nevening. With this, increasing mid level moisture wil allow for \nincreasing cloudy cover from southwest to northeast late this \nafternoon through this evening. Otherwise for today, near \nfreezing high temperatures in the upper 20s to lower 30s are \nbroadly expected this afternoon. With prevailing cloud cover, \nlow temperatures are forecast to remain mostly in the teens. \n\nThe aforementioned closed trough is then expected to dive down \nacross the northern Plains late Monday night through much of \nTuesday. The overall potential for precpitation still appears to\nbe low (10 to 30 percent) with this system as the near surface \nlayer remains well and dry through this period, with some \nlimited light snow showers possible over the far southern James \nRiver Valley into southeastern North Dakota Tuesday afternoon. \nOverall accumulations are expected to be very light, with a low \nchance for dusting to a few tenths of an inch currently \nanticipated. With surface winds turning southwesterly behind \nthis system, highs on Tuesday are forecast from the mid 20s east\nto the mid 30s west. \n\nSouth westerly to near zonal flow over the northern Plains \nWednesday and Thursday will continue to promote warming and dry \nweather across the forecast area, with highs in the mid 30s to \nlower 40s on Wednesday, then broadly in the 40s for \nThanksgiving. Looking ahead to Friday into the weekend, some \nopportunities for precpitation begin to crop as a series of \nnorthern stream shortwave pass through the region. While \ninitially still warm, with highs broadly in the 40s and 50s on \nFriday, overnight low temperatures are still anticipated to drop\nto or below freezing. Thus, a transition from rain to snow \nwould be expected to occurring if and when precpitation does \nend up falling overnight. Breezy to windy conditions can also be\nanticipated during this period as strong H7 jet lifts across \nthe region and winds are mixed down by steepening low level \nlapse rates. Cooler, more seasonal temperatures mainly in the \n30s can then be anticipated through the remainder of the \nweekend, though some 40s may linger in the far southwest.",
